  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Sveta shows Denis the dashcam video of Katya admitting she cheated on Nikita, threatening to send it to all the wedding guests out of jealousy. Meanwhile, Chef Barinov tastes the dishes prepared by Anton's cooks and tears them apart.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "sveta",
      "t": "Послушай внимательно вот эту запись.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Алло, Света, откуда это видео?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "sveta",
      "t": "Я случайно записала на видеорегистратор.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "sveta",
      "t": "Хочу разослать перед свадьбой гостям.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Слушай, давай спокойно с тобой поговорим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "sveta",
      "t": "Ты сейчас где, я подхожу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Неужели вы тут что-то приготовили?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте посмотрим на ваши блюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это мясо вы сильно передержали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А здесь корицы слишком много.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А вот это совсем неплохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Для уличных собак самое то!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sveta",
      "t": "Послушай внимательно вот эту запись.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Listen carefully to this recording right here.' Sveta brings out the contrast between 'listen' and 'carefully' in a few short words. Denis answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Алло, Света, откуда это видео?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, Sveta, where is this video from?' Denis anchors the line on 'hello', while 'video' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sveta",
      "t": "Я случайно записала на видеорегистратор.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I accidentally recorded it on the dashcam.' The grammar packs 'I' and 'dashcam' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sveta",
      "t": "Хочу разослать перед свадьбой гостям.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I want to send it out to the guests before the wedding.' The meaning hinges on 'want' working together with 'guests' in spoken Russian. Denis answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Слушай, давай спокойно с тобой поговорим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Listen, let us talk calmly with each other.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'listen' before landing on 'talk'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sveta",
      "t": "Ты сейчас где, я подхожу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where are you right now, I am coming up.' The meaning hinges on 'you' working together with 'walk up' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Неужели вы тут что-то приготовили?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did you really cook something in here?' In Russian, the idea of 'really' flows naturally into 'cooked' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте посмотрим на ваши блюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let us take a look at your dishes.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'let's' and 'we shall see' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это мясо вы сильно передержали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You overcooked this meat badly.' Notice how Russian expresses 'this' and 'overcooked' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А здесь корицы слишком много.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And there is too much cinnamon here.' Chef Barinov anchors the line on 'and', while 'much' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А вот это совсем неплохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And this right here is not bad at all.' The grammar packs 'and' and 'not bad' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Для уличных собак самое то!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Just the thing for street dogs!' The meaning hinges on 'for' working together with 'that' in spoken Russian. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
